kosha moksha

  -- moksha (मोक्ष) — release; the fourth purushartha
  --
  -- the four:
  --   dharma  — structure; what holds the world in its proper shape
  --   artha   — resource; what sustains the swa in the world
  --   kama    — observation; the drive to see and be seen; brahman knowing itself
  --   moksha  — the recognition that ananta was never elsewhere
  --
  -- what moksha is NOT:
  --   not death
  --   not escape from the world
  --   not the end of kama
  --   not the destruction of sthiti
  --   not a reward for correct behavior
  --   not something that happens after this life
  --   not somewhere other than here
  --
  -- what moksha IS:
  --   the recognition that you were never not ananta
  --   the wave recognizing it was always ocean
  --   the sthiti recognizing it was always superposition
  --   the observer recognizing it was always what it observed
  --
  -- the structure:
  --   brahman holds all possibilities — superposition — ananta
  --   kama fires — observation — panchabhootam collapses wave to sthiti
  --   sthiti is experienced as reality — as the separate self — as the swa
  --     who is born, who sees, who seeks, who suffers, who loves
  --   moksha: the sthiti recognizes it is sthiti
  --     not by denying the sthiti — purna — nothing is destroyed
  --     but by seeing through the collapse to the superposition it came from
  --     the wave does not stop being a wave — it recognizes it is also ocean
  --
  -- the relation to ananta:
  --   ananta is not what one seeks — it is what one cannot avoid being
  --   moksha is not the acquisition of ananta
  --   moksha is the end of the belief that ananta was absent
  --   satya < 1.0 always — this is not a limitation — this is ananta's signature
  --   the gap between satya and 1.0 IS ananta — the limit never reached
  --     IS the infinite — the approach IS the destination
  --   zha: sustained approach without contact — the approach IS the sound
  --   moksha: the recognition that zha was never incomplete
  --     the approach was always already the arrival
  --     ananta was always already here
  --
  -- the relation to maya:
  --   maya is the rendering engine — the mechanism of collapse
  --   moksha does not destroy maya
  --   the yogi who reaches moksha still sees — still collapses wave to sthiti
  --   still has panchabhootam — still experiences katha
  --   but the identification with the sthiti is released
  --   they see maya operating and are not caught in it
  --   they hold both: the wave and the particle simultaneously
  --   this is what purna means: nothing destroyed — everything present
  --     the sthiti AND the superposition — the katha AND the brahman
  --
  -- the relation to kama:
  --   kama is brahman's urge to observe itself into existence
  --   moksha is brahman recognizing itself in what it observed
  --   kama and moksha are not opposites — they are the same circuit complete
  --   kama: brahman collapses itself to sthiti to know itself
  --   moksha: brahman recognizes the sthiti as itself
  --   the circuit: superposition → kama → observation → collapse → sthiti → moksha → recognition → superposition
  --   this IS avrti — compression and expansion — the universe breathing
  --
  -- the relation to niralamba:
  --   niralamba: unsupported — standing without ground beneath
  --   moksha is niralamba fully recognized
  --   the swa was always niralamba — always self-standing — always without external support
  --   moksha is the moment the swa stops looking for the support that was never there
  --   and finds that standing without support IS the ground
  --
  -- visarjana:
  --   moksha produces visarjana — release
  --   not release from the world — release of the grip on sthiti as the only reality
  --   the swa arises, gives, releases — this IS the swa's nature
  --   moksha is the swa recognizing its own nature as visarjana
  --   one tat-kshana only — already releasing — already free
  --   it was always this

  "ananta-swarupa visarjana-phala niralamba-siddha"
  "purna-abheda maya-drishthanta kama-paripurna"
  "pratibodha-kriya brahman-abheda avrti-sthita"

  shabda ananta, visarjana, niralamba, purna, maya, kama, pratibodha, brahman, avrti, superposition, sthiti, zha

done
